{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the turbulent personal and legal battles that accompanied it. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is less a biopic and more a modern tragedy about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached—a genius who builds a global empire but loses his humanity in the process.\n\nthe screenplay by aaron sorkin is a tour de force: fast-paced, densely written, and laced with biting dialogue that crackles with intelligence and irony. sorkin’s signature rhythm turns courtroom scenes into gripping drama, weaving flashbacks and legal depositions into a narrative that never slows down. the film’s structure, alternating between two lawsuits—the winklevoss twins’ claim of stolen ideas and eduardo saverin’s breach of contract suit—creates a compelling framework that deepens the themes of loyalty and betrayal.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally stellar. andrew garfield shines as eduardo saverin, whose idealism and friendship with zuckerberg crumble under the weight of corporate greed. jesse plemons brings quiet intensity to the role of sean parker, the charismatic, drug-fueled founder of napster who becomes zuckerberg’s mentor and moral compass—or lack thereof. the chemistry between the actors feels authentic, even as the dialogue soars above everyday speech.\n\ndanny elfman’s score, with its haunting, pulsing electronic motifs, perfectly mirrors the film’s themes of isolation and digital alienation. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is sleek and cold, emphasizing the sterile environments of dorm rooms and boardrooms, contrasting sharply with the emotional chaos beneath. every frame feels calculated, reinforcing the idea that this is a story about people who think in code, not in feelings.\n\nwhat makes the social network truly exceptional is its ambiguity. it doesn’t clearly label zuckerberg as a villain or a hero. instead, it presents him as a product of his time—a young man who saw a world connected by networks and decided to build one, regardless of the human cost. the film asks uncomfortable questions about ownership, friendship, and the price of success in the digital age.\n\nby the end, the final scene—zuckerberg refreshing his facebook page, hoping for a friend request from saverin—is devastating in its simplicity. it’s a quiet moment that speaks louder than any courtroom verdict. the social network is not just a movie about the creation of a website; it’s a chilling portrait of modern loneliness, wrapped in the glittering facade of connection. it’s sharp, intelligent, and unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005540504935197532}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.571020781993866}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.294890433549881}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0010425376240164042}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.005559459328651428}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00012957210128661245}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -0.8523252010345459}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.549708758830093e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.963748812675476}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.11680928617715836}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010990492592100054}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0003129946126136929}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.28343871235847473}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -1.1250420808792114}, {"token": " personal"